## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a runtime-extensible plugin system with clear interfaces, error handling, and concurrency controls for a distributed task system.

## Key Concepts

- Command pattern with pluggable handlers
- Runtime registry / plugin loader
- Retry policy with backoff
- Global rate limiting and concurrency control
- Runtime loading without restart

## Code Example

```typescript
interface CommandHandler<T> {
  execute(payload: T): Promise<void>;
}

type Registry<T> = Map<string, CommandHandler<T>>;

interface RetryPolicy {
  maxRetries: number;
  backoffMs: number;
}

class Dispatcher<T> {
  private registry: Registry<T> = new Map();
  private policies: Map<string, RetryPolicy> = new Map();
  // pseudo semaphore for rate limiting
  async dispatch(name: string, payload: T): Promise<void> {
    const handler = this.registry.get(name);
    if (!handler) throw new Error('Handler not found');
    const policy = this.policies.get(name) ?? { maxRetries: 0, backoffMs: 0 };
    let attempt = 0;
    while (true) {
      try {
        await handler.execute(payload);
        return;
      } catch (e) {
        if (attempt >= policy.maxRetries) throw e;
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, policy.backoffMs));
        attempt++;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test runtime-loaded handlers?
- How would you handle type-safety for diverse payload schemas across handlers?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of runtime extensibility, plugin architecture, and composition patterns in a practical context.

## Key Concepts
- **Pipeline vs Chain of Responsibility:** ordered transformation versus conditional delegation
- Immutable registries for thread-safety
- Per-item isolation and error containment across stages

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Formatter {
  transform(line: string): string;
}

class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, () => Formatter>();

  register(name: string, factory: () => Formatter): void {
    this.map.set(name, factory);
  }

  create(name: string): Formatter | undefined {
    const factory = this.map.get(name);
    return factory ? factory() : undefined;
  }
}

class Pipeline {
  private formatters: Formatter[] = [];

  use(formatter: Formatter): this {
    this.formatters.push(formatter);
    return this;
  }

  execute(line: string): string {
    return this.formatters.reduce(
      (acc, formatter) => formatter.transform(acc),
      line
    );
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('timestamp', () => ({
  transform: (line) => `[${Date.now()}] ${line}`
}));
registry.register('emoji', () => ({
  transform: (line) => `${line} 🪵`
}));

const pipeline = new Pipeline();
pipeline.use(registry.create('timestamp')!);
pipeline.use(registry.create('emoji')!);

console.log(pipeline.execute('log message'));
// Output: [1712345678900] log message 🪵
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a runtime-extendable, observable notification system using plug‑in sinks and safe parallel dispatch.

## Key Concepts

- Runtime registries
- Plugin/Strategy patterns
- Concurrency and backoff
- Idempotency and deduplication
